#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial shows how to use [SQOA Solver, a Qiskit Function by "
"JIJ](/guides/jij-sqoa-solver), to solve a maximum independent set (MIS) "
"problem with more binary variables than the selected QPU has qubits. The "
"basic steps are to load an MIS instance from the [Quantum Optimization "
"Benchmarking Library (QOBLIB)](https://github.com/ZIB-AOPT/QOBLIB) "
"[\\[1\\]](#references), formulate it in quadratic unconstrained binary "
"optimization (QUBO) form with [qiskit-addon-opt-"
"mapper](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-opt-mapper/), submit it to "
"SQOA Solver for execution on an IBM&reg; QPU, and validate the returned "
"solution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The maximum independent set problem {#the-maximum-independent-set-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An independent set is a subset of nodes in a graph such that no two selected"
" nodes share an edge. A maximum independent set (MIS) is an independent set "
"with the largest possible number of nodes. Applications of MIS include "
"scheduling, resource allocation, and network design.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"MIS is NP-hard. In an exhaustive search, every additional node doubles the "
"number of subsets to consider, so this approach is impractical even for "
"moderately sized graphs. QOBLIB includes MIS as one of its ten problem "
"classes, so it is a useful benchmark for evaluating quantum optimization "
"workflows on real hardware.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematical formulation {#mathematical-formulation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Given an undirected graph $G = (V, E)$, assign a binary variable $x_v$ to "
"each node $v$:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$x_v = 1$ if node $v$ is selected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$x_v = 0$ otherwise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The [binary linear formulation](https://github.com/ZIB-"
"AOPT/QOBLIB/tree/main/07-independentset/models/binary_linear) maximizes the "
"number of selected nodes while preventing both endpoints of any edge from "
"being selected:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\max_{x} \\quad & \\sum_{v \\in V} x_v \\\\\n"
"\\text{subject to} \\quad & x_v + x_w \\leq 1 && \\forall (v,w) \\in E, \\\\\n"
"& x_v \\in \\{0,1\\} && \\forall v \\in V.\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The objective counts the selected nodes. Together, the edge constraints "
"ensure that the selected nodes form an independent set.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses QOBLIB's [binary unconstrained "
"formulation](https://github.com/ZIB-"
"AOPT/QOBLIB/tree/main/07-independentset/models/binary_unconstrained), which "
"replaces the edge constraints with quadratic penalties:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\max_{x \\in \\{0,1\\}^{|V|}} \\quad \\sum_{v \\in V} x_v - 2 \\sum_{(v,w) \\in E} x_v x_w.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Whenever both endpoints of an edge are selected, the second term subtracts "
"two from the objective. This penalty makes a conflicting selection less "
"favorable than keeping either endpoint alone. The resulting objective is a "
"QUBO, which is the input format that SQOA Solver accepts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why use SQOA Solver? {#why-use-sqoa-solver}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Many quantum optimization approaches assign one qubit to each binary "
"variable, so the capacity of the quantum hardware can directly limit the "
"problem size. SQOA Solver combines several reduction and hybrid-computing "
"techniques to ease this restriction:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**It can tackle problems with more binary variables than the QPU has "
"qubits.** Presolve applies reduction rules that can fix variables and "
"simplify the remaining QUBO while preserving at least one optimal solution. "
"Empirically, presolve can also make the remaining QUBO sparser, which helps "
"the QRAC-based encoding that follows it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The solver then uses a QRAC-based encoding derived from Quantum Random "
"Access Optimization (QRAO) [\\[3\\]](#references) to represent multiple "
"variables (up to three) with no direct QUBO interaction on the same qubit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The reductions from presolve and encoding depend on the problem structure. For example, in this tutorial, a 200-variable problem uses only 67 qubits.\n"
"* **It can be less sensitive to hardware noise.** Sample-based quantum diagonalization [\\[4\\]](#references), [\\[5\\]](#references) uses the QPU to sample bitstrings but performs the diagonalization classically. This avoids repeated QPU evaluations for variational parameter updates and can reduce the impact of noise on the optimization workflow.\n"
"* **It automates the workflow from presolve through post-processing.** You provide a correctly formatted QUBO instance and select a backend. SQOA Solver then runs the whole workflow automatically. It applies presolve, maps the classical variables to a relaxed quantum problem, constructs and transpiles the sampling circuits, performs quantum sampling and classical diagonalization, rounds and refines the assignment, and reconstructs and evaluates the classical solution.\n"
msgstr ""

