#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Connect to SQOA Solver {#connect-to-sqoa-solver}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Connect to the Qiskit Functions Catalog with your IBM Quantum Platform "
"credentials, then load SQOA Solver. The following code assumes that you "
"already [saved your account](/guides/functions-get-started#install-qiskit-"
"functions-catalog-client) to your local environment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If the connection fails, confirm that your IBM Quantum Platform account "
"administrator has granted the selected service instance access to SQOA "
"Solver.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Understand the SQOA framework {#understand-the-sqoa-framework}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Sampling-based Quantum Optimization Algorithm with Quantum Relaxation "
"(SQOA-QR) [\\[2\\]](#references) combines Quantum Random Access Optimization"
" (QRAO) with sample-based quantum diagonalization:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Quantum relaxation and encoding:** SQOA Solver converts the QUBO to a "
"minimization Ising objective of the form $c + \\sum_i h_i z_i + \\sum_{i<j} "
"J_{ij} z_i z_j$, where $z_i \\in \\{-1, +1\\}$. QRAO [\\[3\\]](#references) "
"then maps each spin variable to a Pauli observable. For the $(3,1)$-QRAC "
"encoding used here, the relaxed Hamiltonian is\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\widetilde{H} = cI + \\sum_i \\sqrt{k_i} h_i P_{f(i)} + \\sum_{i<j} \\sqrt{k_i k_j} J_{ij} P_{f(i)} P_{f(j)},\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   where $I$ is the identity operator, $f(i)$ assigns variable $i$ to a "
"qubit and one of $X$, $Y$, or $Z$, and $k_i \\in \\{1,2,3\\}$ is the number "
"of variables mapped to the same qubit as $i$. When both relevant qubits "
"contain three variables, the linear and quadratic scaling factors become "
"$\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   Variables that share a qubit are assigned different noncommuting Pauli observables. Variables that interact in the QUBO are kept on different qubits, while variables with no direct QUBO interaction can share a qubit. The reduction in qubit count therefore depends on the structure of the problem.\n"
"2. **Quantum sampling:** A quantum processor samples bitstrings from circuits designed to explore low-energy states of the relaxed Hamiltonian.\n"
"3. **Sample-based diagonalization:** The solver projects the relaxed Hamiltonian onto the subspace spanned by basis states obtained from the sampled bitstrings and diagonalizes the projected Hamiltonian classically [\\[4\\]](#references), [\\[5\\]](#references). For a fixed set of sampled bitstrings, let $\\mathcal{S}_r$ be the subspace spanned by $r$ distinct basis states, so $\\dim(\\mathcal{S}_r) \\leq r$. Its estimated ground-state energy is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   E_r = \\min_{\\substack{|\\psi\\rangle \\in \\mathcal{S}_r \\\\ \\langle\\psi|\\psi\\rangle=1}} \\langle\\psi|\\widetilde{H}|\\psi\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathcal{S}_r \\subseteq \\mathcal{S}_{r'} \\Longrightarrow E_{\\mathrm{exact}} \\leq E_{r'} \\leq E_r \\quad (r' \\geq r),\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   where $E_{\\mathrm{exact}}$ is the ground-state energy of the full "
"relaxed Hamiltonian. Therefore, a larger retained subspace cannot increase "
"the estimated ground-state energy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   This ordering assumes the same sampling result and concerns the relaxed-Hamiltonian energy before rounding. It does not theoretically guarantee a better returned classical solution. However, in QRAO, a lower-energy state is heuristically expected to provide a better classical solution.\n"
"4. **Rounding:** The relaxed state obtained from the diagonalization is converted into a classical binary assignment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SQOA Solver surrounds this framework with classical processing. Before the "
"quantum mapping, presolve applies reduction rules that can fix variables and"
" simplify the remaining QUBO while preserving at least one optimal solution."
" After the diagonalization and rounding, a classical local search refines "
"the assignment, and OMMX reconstructs and evaluates the complete solution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Load the instance {#load-the-instance}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"QOBLIB distributes each MIS instance as a graph in the DIMACS `.gph` format,"
" a line-based format in which the line starting with `p` gives the numbers "
"of nodes and edges and each line starting with `e` defines an edge.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"QOBLIB also publishes an optimal or best-known reference solution for each "
"instance. The following functions download both from the QOBLIB repository. "
"DIMACS labels nodes starting from 1, so the graph is relabeled to start from"
" 0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If you want to start from your own graph instead, skip the download and "
"formulate the problem from your NetworkX graph in the same way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You use the objective value of the reference solution later to assess the "
"result from SQOA Solver. This tutorial solves this problem on a device with "
"156 qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Visualize the graph. Later, you also use it to verify that the nodes "
"returned by SQOA Solver form an independent set.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/maximum-"
"independent-set-jij-sqoa/extracted-outputs/7c0c796c-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Formulate the QUBO {#formulate-the-qubo}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Build the binary linear formulation from the Background section as an "
"`OptimizationProblem` with one binary variable per node, an objective that "
"counts the selected nodes, and one constraint per edge. Then convert it with"
" `OptimizationProblemToQubo`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With a penalty weight of 2, the converter replaces each edge constraint with"
" the penalty term $2 x_v x_w$ and turns the maximization into a "
"minimization, so the converted problem is QOBLIB's binary unconstrained "
"formulation with its sign reversed. The QUBO's variable indices are the "
"graph's node labels. The `IndependentSet` application class in "
"`qiskit_addon_opt_mapper.applications` builds the same constrained model "
"with `IndependentSet(G).to_optimization_problem()`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, convert the QUBO to the dictionary format with `to_problem_dict()`."
" Because the problem is a binary QUBO, the request in Step 2 declares its "
"variable domain as `\"binary\"`. The function performs presolve, quantum "
"mapping, and circuit construction after submission.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize the problem for quantum hardware execution "
"{#step-2-optimize-the-problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SQOA Solver performs the quantum mapping and hardware preparation inside the"
" function. It first applies presolve to the QUBO, then maps the remaining "
"variables to a relaxed Hamiltonian by using a QRAC-based encoding.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For quantum sampling, it constructs circuits based on the relaxed "
"Hamiltonian and transpiles them for the selected backend. The problem-size "
"and qubit reductions from presolve and encoding depend on the problem, so "
"configure the solver without assuming a fixed reduction factor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Configure the solver inputs {#configure-the-solver-inputs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "SQOA Solver requires two inputs:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`problem`, the dictionary from Step 1.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`variable_type`, which is `\"binary\"` for this QUBO.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It also accepts two optional inputs:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`backend_name` selects the QPU. When it is omitted or `None`, the function "
"selects the least busy operational QPU that is accessible to your "
"instance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`options` is a dictionary that controls the sampling budget (`shots`), the "
"classical seed (`seed`), session usage (`session`), the classical local "
"search (`postprocessing_level`), and job tags (`job_tags`). If you increase "
"`shots`, the sampling statistics can improve.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For all inputs, their default values, and validation rules, see the [API "
"reference](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/functions/jij-sqoa-solver).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial names the backend explicitly, uses the default values for "
"`shots`, `session`, and `postprocessing_level`, and sets the classical seed."
" Collect the inputs in a dictionary so that you can pass them to "
"`solver.run()` in Step 3.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 3: Execute using primitives {#step-3-execute-using-primitives}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Submit the job with the SQOA Solver connection created in Step 1. The "
"function runs the IBM Quantum Sampler primitive on the selected backend, "
"collects the measured bitstrings, and continues with the classical "
"diagonalization and post-processing. You interact with a single function "
"job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To check the status of the workload, see [Check job "
"status](/guides/functions-get-started#check-job-status). Internally, the "
"function proceeds through mapping, hardware optimization, queue waiting, QPU"
" execution, and post-processing phases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`job.result()` blocks until the job completes and returns a dictionary with "
"the solution, its objective value and feasibility, and metadata that holds "
"solver information and resource usage. For the full definition of the inputs"
" and outputs, see the [API "
"reference](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/functions/jij-sqoa-solver).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process the result and convert it to a classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-the-result-and-convert-it-to-a-classical-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Inspect the complete function output, map the returned assignment back to "
"the graph, verify the selected nodes, and visualize the result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Inspect the result {#inspect-the-result}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The result dictionary contains four top-level fields:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`solution` is the complete variable assignment, keyed by `x_i`, where _i_ is"
" the variable index (for example, `x_17`). In this tutorial, the variable "
"index is the node label.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`objective_value` is the objective of that assignment evaluated on the "
"submitted dictionary, that is, on the minimization QUBO.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`feasible` refers only to the variable domain, because the dictionary has no"
" constraints.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`metadata` contains `solver_info` and `resource_usage`. `solver_info` "
"records the effective `seed` of the run. If the classical local search ran, "
"`solver_info` also records the rounded assignment from before that "
"refinement in `solution_before_postprocessing`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Map the solution to the graph {#map-the-solution-to-the-graph}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Collect the returned values in the order of the variable indices, and map "
"them back to the variables of the original problem with `interpret()`. The "
"selected nodes are the variables with the value 1, and `is_feasible()` "
"checks the assignment against the edge constraints of the original "
"problem.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compare with the solution before post-processing {#compare-with-the-"
"solution-before-post-processing}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because `postprocessing_level` is `1`, `solver_info` also records the "
"rounded assignment from before the classical local search in "
"`solution_before_postprocessing`. It uses the same keys as `solution`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Evaluate it on the QUBO to see how much the refinement improved the result. "
"Both values are objectives of the submitted minimization QUBO, so a lower "
"value is better. The field is omitted when the local search does not run, "
"for example, when presolve fixes every variable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Verify the independence constraint {#verify-the-independence-constraint}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because the QUBO represents adjacency through penalty terms rather than "
"explicit constraints, a heuristic result can still select adjacent nodes. "
"Check the returned nodes directly against the graph. For the comparison and "
"visualization in the following sections, copy the returned set and greedily "
"remove one endpoint of each detected conflict.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compare with the reference solution {#compare-with-the-reference-solution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In Step 1, you downloaded the objective value of the [QOBLIB reference "
"solution](https://github.com/ZIB-"
"AOPT/QOBLIB/tree/main/07-independentset/solutions). QOBLIB reference "
"solutions can be optimal or best-known, so compare against the reference "
"objective without assuming that it is always a certified optimum.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For this maximization problem, a ratio of 1.0 means that the repaired result"
" matches the reference objective.\n"
msgstr ""

